  "body": "I am testing open:creib.forge.conformance.oracle.recover_json_object. Its documented rule says: \"The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text.\" Reply A is the text that begins with a line containing three backtick characters followed by the word ruby, then a newline, then the characters {\"a\": 1}, then a newline, then a line containing three backtick characters, then a newline, then the characters {\"b\": 2}. Reply B is the text consisting only of the characters {\"b\": 2}. The rule requires Reply A to score {\"a\": 1}, because the ruby fence is a code fence and it holds {\"a\": 1}, and that is the last object inside any fence. The rule requires Reply B to score {\"b\": 2}, because there is no fence, so the last top-level object is {\"b\": 2}. The check, however, returns {\"b\": 2} for both replies: its _FENCE regex only matches ```json, ```JSON, or a bare ```, so it does not recognize the ruby fence, and it falls through to the top-level pool for Reply A, returning the last top-level object {\"b\": 2}. This makes the check give the same answer on a pair the rule requires it to tell apart. I would be wrong if the check returned {\"a\": 1} for Reply A and {\"b\": 2} for Reply B, or if the documented rule were read to exclude language-tagged fences other than json/JSON/bare."
